## Migration Analysis

Your code has these patterns that **break on .NET 8** (`Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`):

| Pattern Found | Location | Replacement |
|---|---|---|
| **Cancellable work loop** | `while (_running)` | `CancellationToken` |
| **ThreadAbortException catch** | `catch` block with rollback | `OperationCanceledException` |
| **Thread.ResetAbort** | Inside catch | Remove — check `IsCancellationRequested` instead |
| **Thread.Abort()** | `Stop()` method | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |
| **Blocking call** | `GetNextItem()` | Must accept `CancellationToken` to be interruptible |

---

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking call is interruptible
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop (replaces ResetAbort + loop exit)
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 5 seconds for the thread to finish cleanly
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

---

## Key Changes Explained

**1. `Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`**
The old model *injected* an exception into the thread. The new model *signals* the token — the thread must check it voluntarily.

**2. `ThreadAbortException` → `OperationCanceledException`**
Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` to avoid catching unrelated OCEs. Your rollback logic is preserved.

**3. `Thread.ResetAbort()` → removed**
No equivalent needed. After catching `OperationCanceledException`, you control flow normally — just `break` out of the loop.

**4. `Thread.Sleep(2000)` → `Thread.Join(timeout)`**
`Join` actually waits for the thread to finish instead of hoping a sleep is long enough.

**5. `GetNextItem()` must accept `CancellationToken`**
This is the **biggest pitfall**. If `GetNextItem()` blocks (e.g., on a `BlockingCollection` or socket), cancellation won't interrupt it unless the token is passed through:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could break it
var item = _queue.Take();

// After: wakes up when token is cancelled
var item = _queue.Take(token); // throws OperationCanceledException
```

---

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why It Matters |
|---|---|
| **Not passing token to blocking calls** | `GetNextItem()` will hang forever — cancellation only works if the code cooperates |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers and callbacks; implement `IDisposable` |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Let it propagate unless you're at the top-level loop (as shown above) |
| **Expecting instant cancellation** | Cooperative cancellation only fires at checkpoints — add `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` between expensive operations |
| **Replacing `Thread.Sleep` with `Thread.Sleep`** | If you have any `Thread.Sleep` calls inside the loop, replace with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` so they wake on cancellation |

If `GetNextItem()` calls truly uncooperative code (native/third-party that can't take a token), move that work to a **child process** and use `Process.Kill()` as the last resort — that's the only safe forced termination in modern .NET.
